# Predicting Extreme Rainfall Days in Copenhagen

A machine learning project forecasting the risk of extreme rainfall
("cloudburst") events in Copenhagen, using 10 years of historical
weather data.

## Why this framing (not just "temperature forecasting")

Plain temperature forecasting is the generic, tutorial-shaped version
of this idea. Extreme rainfall prediction is more interesting and more
defensible because:

- It's **civically relevant to Copenhagen specifically** — the city's
  2011 cloudburst caused ~6 billion DKK in damage and led to the
  Copenhagen Cloudburst Management Plan. This is a real problem the
  city has invested in.
- It's a genuinely harder ML problem: extreme events are **rare**
  (~2% of days here), so naive accuracy is meaningless and you have to
  reason properly about class imbalance, calibration, and the right
  metrics (ROC-AUC / PR-AUC / Brier score, not accuracy).
- It forces you to beat a real, honest baseline (**climatology**: "the
  seasonal historical rate") rather than a strawman, which is the
  standard meteorologists actually hold ML models to.

## Data

Pulled from the [Open-Meteo Historical Weather API](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/historical-weather-api)
(free, no key required), which serves ERA5/ERA5-Land reanalysis data.
Coordinates: Copenhagen (55.6761° N, 12.5683° E), 2015–2024.

Daily variables: max/min/mean temperature, precipitation, wind speed
and gusts, mean surface pressure, mean relative humidity, mean cloud
cover.

## Method

1. **`data_fetch.py`** — pulls the raw daily series from Open-Meteo,
   plus hourly precipitation reduced to a daily max-1-hour value.
2. **`features.py`** — builds the supervised dataset:
   - target: whether *tomorrow* sees a skybrud-style extreme rain
     hour (≥15mm in a single hour — the closer proxy to how Denmark
     actually defines cloudbursts, falling back to a 20mm/24h total
     if hourly data isn't available)
   - seasonal (cyclical day-of-year) encoding
   - **wind U/V decomposition**: raw speed+direction can't tell the
     model whether moisture is arriving off the Baltic/North Sea vs.
     across drier continental Europe — decomposing into zonal (U) and
     meridional (V) components gives it a continuous, physically
     meaningful signal instead
   - **14-day moisture deficit**: rolling precipitation sum minus a
     simple temperature-based evaporative-demand proxy, as a rough
     pre-saturation indicator (explicitly *not* a full Penman-Monteith
     calculation, which would need solar radiation data this project
     doesn't pull — a limitation worth naming, not hiding)
   - 1/2/3-day lag features for all key variables
   - 3- and 7-day rolling means/sums (is the atmosphere trending
     wetter?)
   - 1- and 3-day pressure change (pressure drops are a classic storm
     precursor)
3. **`model.py`** — trains and compares:
   - a **climatology baseline** (seasonal historical rate, smoothed
     over a ±7-day window) — the bar the model has to clear
   - a **calibrated gradient-boosted classifier**
     (`HistGradientBoostingClassifier` wrapped in
     `CalibratedClassifierCV`): `class_weight="balanced"` fixes the
     ranking problem from class imbalance but distorts the raw
     probabilities toward 0/1, so isotonic calibration maps them back
     to realistic frequencies
   - a **stacked model**: climatology + ML predictions fed into a
     logistic regression, so the ML model effectively learns a
     *residual correction* on top of the seasonal norm
   - **purged time-series cross-validation** (5 folds, with a 14-day
     gap purged around each split) used for model-stability checking,
     plus a genuinely held-out final year (2024) for the headline
     numbers — a plain time-ordered split still leaks a little,
     because lag/rolling features near a fold boundary are computed
     from data straddling train and validation; purging removes that.

### Scoping note

This list started from a much longer set of ideas (CAPE/synoptic
features from Copernicus, DMI forecast-residual correction, an LSTM
comparison, automated pipelines, a dashboard). Those are real,
legitimate next steps — but they were left out on purpose: the goal
of this project is depth you can defend in an interview, not breadth.
Everything above is something you built and can explain line by line.
See "Natural extensions" below for what's worth mentioning as future
work rather than building now.

## How to run it

```bash
pip install requests pandas numpy scikit-learn
python data_fetch.py      # pulls real data from Open-Meteo, ~10s
python features.py        # builds the feature table
python model.py           # trains baseline + model, prints metrics
```

## What to say about this in your application

- **The honest result, not an inflated one.** On synthetic test data
  the gradient-boosted model beat the climatology baseline on ROC-AUC
  (0.63 vs 0.57) but the gap on rare-event precision was still modest
  — extreme-event forecasting is hard, and admitting that (with the
  right metrics to back it up) reads as more credible than claiming
  a suspiciously good accuracy number. Run it on the real Open-Meteo
  data and report your actual numbers.
- **Why the modeling choices were deliberate**, not just "the results":
  smoothed climatology baseline, time-based split, class imbalance
  handling, permutation importance for interpretability (since
  `HistGradientBoostingClassifier` has no built-in feature importances
  — you had to know to reach for `sklearn.inspection.permutation_importance`
  instead).
- **The connection to your existing math background**: this is a
  probabilistic/statistical modeling problem — smoothing, baselines,
  calibration (Brier score) — that draws on the same statistical
  thinking as your stochastic processes study and your Black-Scholes
  EE, applied outside finance.

## Natural extensions if you want to go further

Worth naming in your writeup as deliberate future work, not building
